{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019WpaJVP3dv3uq4UsgzcYka","type":"tool_result","content":"29:**Also flagged:** B3 and B4 disagree on the early spacing ramp. B3 says Ch 3 = 10%, Ch 4 = 15%, then 20–25%. B4's restatement says Ch 4–5 ≈ 10%, Ch 6–8 ≈ 15%, Ch 9–13 ≈ 20%. B3 is the dedicated stage and its schedule is adopted here; B4's table is a generic restatement of the skill's Part 10 example and is superseded.\n50:**The spine.** The book has one structural argument, stated three times at increasing altitude: *the control loop*. Chapter 3 introduces it as the animating idea of the cluster. Chapter 6 instantiates it in the workload controllers. Chapter 15 generalizes it to a Git repository — and that recognition, not a fifth list to memorize, is the book's **primary Zenith**. The whole of Part IV's ordering (packaging → delivery → debugging) exists to set that beat up: Argo CD consumes Helm charts, so Helm must land first.\n102:### Chapter 3 — The Ship's Company\n122:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 3 — control loop, kube-apiserver, etcd as state of record\n154:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]** — from Ch 3–5. Anchors: the control loop (Ch 3) now visible in a named controller; selectors (Ch 4) as the ReplicaSet→Pod join; probes (Ch 5) as what makes a rolling update safe\n182:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 3 (control plane), Ch 4 (objects, namespaces), Ch 7 (nodes as scheduling targets)\n184:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]**, from Ch 3–7, **with the ≥4-back spacing floor now active** — at least one item must come from Ch 2–4. Anchors: the OCI/CRI boundary (Ch 2) or the control loop (Ch 3) as the ≥4-back item; namespaces (Ch 4) under ResourceQuota; node conditions against scheduling (Ch 7)\n287:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 3 (the control loop), Ch 6 (controllers, rolling update mechanics), Ch 14 (Helm charts as a manifest source)\n289:- **Retrieval targets**: **25% — ceiling** **[B3]**, from all previous. ≥4-back floor satisfied several times over. Mandatory anchors: **the control loop (Ch 3)** — the retrieval that *is* the Zenith; rolling-update mechanics (Ch 6) now given strategy vocabulary; Helm charts (Ch 14) as Argo's input; ConfigMap/Secret (Ch 4) as twelve-factor factor III; drift detection against `spec`/`status` (Ch 4)\n414:| 8 | 20% | 3–7 | Namespaces under ResourceQuota; node conditions | **Ch 2 CRI or Ch 3 control loop** |\n421:| 15 | **25%** | all | **Control loop (Ch 3) — the Zenith**; rolling-update mechanics (Ch 6); Helm as Argo input (Ch 14); ConfigMap/Secret as factor III (Ch 4) | Ch 3, 4, 6 |\n438:| Ch 3 → Ch 6 → Ch 15 | The control-loop spine, three beats |","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f1997aae-8523-4efc-9003-7a8d6bc39260","uuid":"86dec582-9042-426f-a825-f374df5d60bf","timestamp":"2026-08-24T06:37:13.512Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"29:**Also flagged:** B3 and B4 disagree on the early spacing ramp. B3 says Ch 3 = 10%, Ch 4 = 15%, then 20–25%. B4's restatement says Ch 4–5 ≈ 10%, Ch 6–8 ≈ 15%, Ch 9–13 ≈ 20%. B3 is the dedicated stage and its schedule is adopted here; B4's table is a generic restatement of the skill's Part 10 example and is superseded.\n50:**The spine.** The book has one structural argument, stated three times at increasing altitude: *the control loop*. Chapter 3 introduces it as the animating idea of the cluster. Chapter 6 instantiates it in the workload controllers. Chapter 15 generalizes it to a Git repository — and that recognition, not a fifth list to memorize, is the book's **primary Zenith**. The whole of Part IV's ordering (packaging → delivery → debugging) exists to set that beat up: Argo CD consumes Helm charts, so Helm must land first.\n102:### Chapter 3 — The Ship's Company\n122:- **Prerequisites**: Ch 3 — control loop, kube-apiserver, etcd as state of record\n154:- **Retrieval targets**: **20%** **[B3]** — from Ch 3–5. Anchors: the control loop (Ch 3) now visible in a named controller; selectors (Ch 4) as the ReplicaS